<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client C++</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-cpp</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over the C++ toolchain. This module is in the reactor only under
        -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for why
        opt-in is load-bearing - and all it does today is build a program that prints one fixture
        line and check that it did (astubbs#242).

        THERE IS NO CMakeLists.txt HERE YET, AND ON THE SOURCE BRANCH THERE IS - the difference
        is deliberate and worth stating, because a reader who knows astubbs/parallel-consumer#293
        will notice. There, the C++ client needs gRPC and protobuf development packages, which is
        why it carries a CMake build and a Dockerfile and builds only inside a container. None of
        that applies to a program that prints one line: C++ has no manifest format, the compiler
        invocation IS the build, and a container would make Docker a prerequisite for scaffolding
        that needs a compiler and nothing else. CMake and the container route arrive with the
        client that needs them.

        -Werror rather than a static analyser: this rung ships no per-language scanners, and the
        compiler's own warnings are free.

        Both commands go through bin/foreign-client-step.sh, which decides what an ABSENT c++
        means: a banner and a skip by default, red under -Dpc.foreign.strict=1, which is what the
        CI row passes. The commands themselves are declared here and nowhere else.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool c++ -- c++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -o target/hello src/hello.cpp</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool c++ --hello cpp -- target/hello</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET HERE, and that is a decision rather than an omission -
                "nothing to clean" and "nobody wired the clean" look identical in a pom. Everything
                this module builds lands under target/, which Maven's default clean already takes.
                the single compiled binary is written straight into target/.
            -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
